  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-367495",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12x)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}-12)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n})}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.65)^{4}}{20(1.65)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.428\\,269…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.428\\,269…)^{4}}{20(1.428\\,269…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.349\\,003…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}}{20{x}_{2}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.349\\,003…)^{4}}{20(1.349\\,003…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.339\\,016…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{15{x}_{3}^{4}}{20{x}_{3}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.339\\,016…)^{4}}{20(1.339\\,016…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.338\\,865….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.34$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.34$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.34,$ rounded to two decimal places.",
